  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 339 PM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nWednesday, an upper-level longwave trough will become established \nacross the Great Lakes and Hudson Bay region, resulting in northwest \nflow aloft across the area. Along the southwestern periphery of this \ntrough, a weak disturbance will move through the region. Surface dew \npoints in the mid to upper 60s, combined with sufficient mid and \nupper-level forcing, will support shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment across portions of the area. In terms of severe weather, \nMUCAPE values of 2000 to 3000 J/kg combined with 40 to 45 knots of \ndeep-layer shear should support organized convection, with large \nhail and damaging winds the primary threats. The SPC currently has \nmuch of the forecast area in a Marginal Risk for severe weather \nWednesday, and this period will continue to be monitored closely \nover the next several forecast cycles. Moisture profiles will also \nremain quite impressive, supporting efficient rainfall processes and \nthe potential for locally heavy rainfall with stronger \nthunderstorms. Temperatures Wednesday will remain on the cooler \nside, with highs generally in the mid to upper 70s across much of \nthe area.\n\nThursday, the longwave trough across the Hudson Bay region will \ncontinue to shift east as an upper-level ridge builds across the \nwestern United States, maintaining northwest flow aloft across the \nregion. Warm air advection will begin to increase as the western \nridge amplifies, with 850-mb temperatures returning to the 20 to 25C \nrange. This will support warmer surface temperatures, with highs \nreturning to the mid 80s across much of the area. Another weak \ndisturbance is forecast to move southeast through the northwest flow \non the backside of the departing longwave trough, potentially \nproviding sufficient lift for scattered showers and thunderstorms \nThursday afternoon and evening. Severe weather may again be possible \ngiven sufficient instability and deep-layer shear. However, \nstrengthening 850-mb warm air advection may result in a stronger \ncap, potentially limiting convective initiation. This period will \ncontinue to be monitored over the next several forecast cycles.\n\nFriday into the weekend, the upper-level ridge across the western \nUnited States will amplify and gradually shift east toward the \nregion. Increasing subsidence associated with the amplifying ridge \nshould favor drier conditions, while the stronger upper-level flow \nand better forcing remain north and east of the area. Temperatures \nwill also trend warmer, with highs returning to the lower to middle \n90s across western portions of the forecast area through Monday, \nwhile north-central Nebraska remains somewhat cooler with highs \ngenerally in the lower to middle 80s. By early next week, the upper-\nlevel ridge is forecast to begin flattening, resulting in a \ntransition toward more zonal flow and potentially allowing stronger \nupper-level flow to return to the region.",
